          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Formal topology is a programme for doing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+mathematics"} finite
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+mathematics"} predicative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
            fashion.
          %p
            It is a kind of
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointless+topology"} pointless topology
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locales"} locales
            rather than
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            (although of course one can recover topological spaces from locales).
          %p
            The basic definitions can be motivated by an attempt to study locales entirely through the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/posites"} posites
            that generate them. However, in order to recover all basic topological notions (particularly those associated with
            %em closed
            rather than
            %em open
            features) predicatively, we need to add a ‘positivity’ relation to the ‘coverage’ relation of sites.
